* **DOI:** [`10.1038/s41586-021-04233-4`](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04233-4)
* **Publisher URL:** [https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-04233-4](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-04233-4)
* **Accessible Full-Text:** [`Coelho_2022_GMGC_Nature_preprint.pdf`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/01_dark_catalogues/Coelho_2022_GMGC_Nature_preprint.pdf) (667 KB) & [`Coelho_2022_GMGC_fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/01_dark_catalogues/Coelho_2022_GMGC_fulltext.xml) (82 KB)
* **Status in `references.bib`:** Already cited (`coelho2022gmgc`).
* **Precise Claim & Limitation for P4:** Clustered 2 billion ORFs into 303 million unigenes across 14 habitats; demonstrated that most genes are habitat-specific and rare.  
  *Limitation:* Cross-sectional pooling cannot distinguish whether rare unigenes are biologically viable, moribund relic DNA, or assembly artifacts. P4 replaces cross-sectional rarity with longitudinal persistence (79.4% detection across 5 expeditions over 31 months).

