### 4. Pavlopoulos et al. (2023) — Global Metagenomic Dark Matter
* **Title:** Unraveling the functional dark matter through global metagenomics
* **Authors / Year:** Georgios A. Pavlopoulos, Fotis A. Baltoumas, Sirui Liu, Oguz Selvitopi, Antonio Pedro Camargo, Stephen Nayfach, Ariful Azad, Simon Roux, Lee Call, Natalia N. Ivanova, I-Min A. Chen, David Paez-Espino, Evangelos Karatzas, Ioannis Iliopoulos, Konstantinos Konstantinidis, James M. Tiedje, Jennifer Pett-Ridge, David Baker, Axel Visel, Christos A. Ouzounis, Sergey Ovchinnikov, Aydin Buluc, Nikos C. Kyrpides (2023)
* **DOI:** [`10.1038/s41586-023-06583-7`](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06583-7)
* **Publisher URL:** [https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06583-7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06583-7)
* **Accessible Full-Text:** [`Pavlopoulos_2023_darkmatter_fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/01_dark_catalogues/Pavlopoulos_2023_darkmatter_fulltext.xml) (207 KB XML via PMC10584684)
* **Status in `references.bib`:** Already cited (`pavlopoulos2023darkmatter`).
* **Precise Claim & Limitation for P4:** Clustered 3 billion proteins from 26,931 metagenomes into 106,198 novel protein families ($\ge 100$ members) with predicted AlphaFold structures.  
  *Limitation:* Enforcing a cutoff of $\ge 100$ global members filters out localized, low-biomass, and ecosystem-specific families. P4 shows that a conservative local admission rule ($\ge 3$ diverged variants, $\ge 3$ samples, $\ge 2$ sites) in a single transect uncovers 106,042 purifying-selected dark families that are fundamentally absent from global databases (28.74% missing from all comparators combined).

